Let $\sigma_i$ be the positive geometric Artin braid crossing in a braid rectangle whose parameter increases from bottom to top, with strand $i$ passing below and strand $i+1$ passing above. Use the bracket chart in which the over-strand runs southwest to northeast, the $A$-smoothing joins left to left and right to right with weight $A$, and the $B$-smoothing joins top to top and bottom to bottom with weight $A^{-1}$. Let $I$ be the through pairing and let $e_i$ be the pairing with a top cap and a bottom cup at positions $i,i+1$. Then the positive crossing has bracket operator $A I+A^{-1}e_i$, and the negative crossing has bracket operator $A^{-1}I+A e_i$.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-0.55,-1)--(-0.55,1);
    \draw[thick] (0.55,-1)--(0.55,1);
    \node at (0,1.35) {$I$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=2.6cm]
    \draw[thick] (-0.55,1) .. controls (0.25,0.55) and (0.25,-0.55) .. (0.55,-1);
    \draw[thick] (0.55,1) .. controls (-0.25,0.55) and (-0.25,-0.55) .. (-0.55,-1);
    \node at (0,1.35) {$e_i$};
  \end{scope}
  \node at (1.25,0) {$\sigma_i=A I+A^{-1}e_i$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Let $A$ be an indeterminate and $n\ge2$. In a vertical $n$-strand braid box
whose longitudinal parameter increases from bottom to top, let $I$ be the
straight tangle and, for
$1\le i<n$, let $e_i$ be the cup-cap tangle with a cap at the top and a cup
at the bottom on strands $i,i+1$, and straight strands elsewhere. Each closed
loop has value $\delta=-A^2-A^{-2}$. For the positive crossing, use positive projection coordinates $(t,x)$, with $t$ longitudinal and $x$ transverse, and over and under tangent vectors $(1,-2h)$ and $(1,2h)$ for $h>0$. Transport the source chart by $(t,x)=(v,-2hu)$: its A-smoothing is the left/right pairing with coefficient $A$, giving $I$, and its B-smoothing is the top/bottom pairing with coefficient $A^{-1}$, giving $e_i$. Set
$$C_i:=AI+A^{-1}e_i.$$
For the opposite crossing, the smoothings exchange their through and cup-cap
pairings; set
$$C_i^{-}:=A^{-1}I+Ae_i.$$
Then $C_iC_i^{-}=C_i^{-}C_i=I$, and for adjacent strands
$$C_iC_{i+1}C_i=C_{i+1}C_iC_{i+1}.$$
Consequently the bracket is unchanged by every local Reidemeister II or III
replacement; arbitrary strand orientations do not change these unoriented
tangle identities.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8, line cap=round]
  \draw (-0.35,-0.25) rectangle (2.35,1.25);
  \foreach \x in {0,1,2} {
    \fill (\x,1) circle (1.4pt);
    \fill (\x,0) circle (1.4pt);
  }
  \draw (0,1) .. controls (0,0.68) and (1,0.68) .. (1,1);
  \draw (0,0) .. controls (0,0.32) and (1,0.32) .. (1,0);
  \draw (2,0) -- (2,1);
  \node[above] at (0,1.06) {$t_1$};
  \node[above] at (1,1.06) {$t_2$};
  \node[above] at (2,1.06) {$t_3$};
  \node[below] at (0,-0.06) {$b_1$};
  \node[below] at (1,-0.06) {$b_2$};
  \node[below] at (2,-0.06) {$b_3$};
  \node at (1,-0.55) {$e_1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The picture names the cap, cup, and through strand that define $e_1$; the
algebraic equalities above are proved below without using the picture as an
inference.

Let $M$ be a smooth oriented $3$-manifold, and put
$R=\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}]$ and $\delta=-A^2-A^{-2}$. A **framed unoriented
link in $M$** is a finite disjoint union of smoothly embedded annuli
$S^1\times[-1,1]$ in the interior of $M$, considered up to ambient isotopy
through such framed annuli; the empty union is allowed. Let
$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{fr}}(M)$ be the set of these isotopy classes and let
$\mathcal F_R(M)$ be the free $R$-module with basis
$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{fr}}(M)$.

For the local crossing relation, choose an orientation-preserving chart from a
closed $3$-ball in $M$ to a standard ball in $\mathbb R^3$, and project to the
first two coordinates. In the displayed chart the over-strand runs from the
southwest endpoint to the northeast endpoint, and the under-strand runs from
the northwest endpoint to the southeast endpoint. The $A$-smoothing pairs the
two left endpoints and the two right endpoints, and the $B$-smoothing pairs
the two top endpoints and the two bottom endpoints. The smoothed strips carry
blackboard framing.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.62,line cap=round,line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.14,0.14);
    \draw[thick] (0.14,-0.14)--(1,-1);
    \draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
    \node at (0,-1.38) {crossing};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=3.0cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.45,0.35) and (-0.45,-0.35) .. (-1,-1);
    \draw[thick] (1,1) .. controls (0.45,0.35) and (0.45,-0.35) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.38) {$A$-smoothing, weight $A$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=6.0cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.35,0.45) and (0.35,0.45) .. (1,1);
    \draw[thick] (-1,-1) .. controls (-0.35,-0.45) and (0.35,-0.45) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.38) {$B$-smoothing, weight $A^{-1}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

For each such local triple, the three framed links agree outside the chart.
Let $\mathcal R_A(M)$ be the submodule of $\mathcal F_R(M)$ generated by
$[L_\times]-A[L_A]-A^{-1}[L_B]$ for all crossing triples, and by
$[L\sqcup O]-\delta[L]$ for every framed link $L$, where $O$ is a
zero-framed trivial circle bounding a disk in a ball disjoint from $L$. The
**Kauffman bracket skein module** of $M$ is
$$
\mathcal K_A(M):=\mathcal F_R(M)/\mathcal R_A(M).
$$
The relations are imposed on framed link classes; no unframed type-I
identification is added.

For the crossingless based unknot diagram, the reduced integral cohomology is $\mathbb Z$ in cohomological degree $0$. For the based closure of the three-crossing negative two-strand braid, with the basepoint on a closure arc and cohomological degree $i=|v|-3$, the reduced integral cohomology is $\mathbb Z$ in degrees $-3,-2,0$ and zero in degree $-1$. In particular, these two specified diagrams have total reduced ranks $1$ and $3$, respectively.

The braid diagram below shows the closure convention: the two outer arcs join corresponding top and bottom endpoints, the braid strands are oriented downward, the two closure arcs are traversed from bottom to top, the three crossings are negative, and the dot marks the basepoint. The top-to-bottom braid word is $\sigma_1^{-3}$, with $\sigma_1^{-1}$ drawn as the left incoming strand passing over the right incoming strand.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.78, every node/.style={font=\small}]
  % Three negative crossings; the left incoming strand is the overstrand.
  \draw[thick] (0.45,3) -- (0.10,2.61);
  \draw[thick] (0.10,2.39) -- (-0.45,2);
  \draw[thick] (-0.45,3) -- (0.45,2);
  \draw[thick] (0.45,2) -- (0.10,1.61);
  \draw[thick] (0.10,1.39) -- (-0.45,1);
  \draw[thick] (-0.45,2) -- (0.45,1);
  \draw[thick] (0.45,1) -- (0.10,0.61);
  \draw[thick] (0.10,0.39) -- (-0.45,0);
  \draw[thick] (-0.45,1) -- (0.45,0);
  % Closure arcs.
  \draw[thick,->] (-0.45,0) .. controls (-1.05,-0.25) and (-1.05,3.25) .. (-0.45,3);
  \draw[thick,->] (0.45,0) .. controls (1.05,-0.25) and (1.05,3.25) .. (0.45,3);
  \fill (-0.88,1.25) circle (2pt);
  \node[left] at (-0.88,1.25) {$p$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The crossing convention in this drawing is explanatory only; the verification computes the resolution cube and does not infer any differential or homology from the picture.

Let $A$ be an indeterminate and let $\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}]$ be the Laurent
polynomial ring in $A$. A **nonempty unoriented regular link diagram** is the
finite planar projection and over/under data of a regular oriented link diagram
after its strand arrows are forgotten. Its finite crossing set is denoted
$C(D)$, and at least one closed component is required. The empty link diagram is
outside this definition.

At a crossing use the following local chart: the over-strand runs from the
southwest endpoint to the northeast endpoint and is drawn continuously over the
under-strand, which runs from northwest to southeast with a gap at the
intersection. Horizontal-right followed by vertical-up is the positive
orientation of this displayed chart. In this chart the **A-smoothing** joins the
two left endpoints and joins the two right endpoints; the **B-smoothing** joins
the two top endpoints and joins the two bottom endpoints. These labels are
attached to this displayed chart and are transported by orientation-preserving
changes of local coordinates. Thus turning right from an entering underpassing
half-strand gives the A-smoothing.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.72, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.14,0.14);
    \draw[thick] (0.14,-0.14)--(1,-1);
    \draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {crossing};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.45,0.35) and (-0.45,-0.35) .. (-1,-1);
    \draw[thick] (1,1) .. controls (0.45,0.35) and (0.45,-0.35) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$A$-smoothing, weight $A$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=6.8cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.35,0.45) and (0.35,0.45) .. (1,1);
    \draw[thick] (-1,-1) .. controls (-0.35,-0.45) and (0.35,-0.45) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$B$-smoothing, weight $A^{-1}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

For a state $S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}$, let $a(S)$ and $b(S)$ be the numbers of
A- and B-smoothings, and let $|S|$ be the number of closed circles in the
crossingless diagram obtained by performing all smoothings. Set
$$\delta:=-A^2-A^{-2}$$
and define the **Kauffman bracket** of $D$ by
$$\langle D\rangle:=\sum_{S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}} A^{a(S)-b(S)}\delta^{|S|-1}\in\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}].$$
Because $D$ is nonempty, every complete smoothing has $|S|\ge1$, so the loop
exponent is nonnegative. A crossingless $k$-component unlink with $k\ge1$ has
bracket $\delta^{k-1}$.
